---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import TrustResponsibilityMap from "../components/diagrams/TrustResponsibilityMap.astro";
import QualificationNotice from "../components/QualificationNotice.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Trust Center | BeaconGuard Assurance"
  description="Review BeaconGuard Result-to-Action Authorization controls for declared enterprise actions, Verify read-only access, evidence provenance, reconstruction and export integrity, and shared deployment responsibility."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Trust Center</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      This Trust Center summarizes how BeaconGuard approaches product security,
      dual authorization, Verify investigation, evidence integrity, and shared
      deployment responsibility. It does not provide legal compliance guarantees
      and does not replace customer compliance, privacy, security, clinical,
      AML, fraud, SIEM, IAM, or GRC programs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <QualificationNotice variant="full" />

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Product security architecture</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard separates Govern, Authorize, and Verify as functional domains.
      Compliance Playbooks become signed immutable release artifacts. Only an
      Active Verified Release governs Request Admission Authorization and
      Result-to-Action Authorization. Runtime outcomes are ALLOW or DENY only.
      Provider success is not enterprise authorization.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Result-to-Action Authorization controls</h2>
    <p>
      After an AI-generated result returns, the enterprise application declares
      the intended enterprise action. BeaconGuard authorizes or denies that
      declared action under the Active Verified Release. The AI-generated result
      remains an untrusted proposal. This is not semantic scoring of model
      response content.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Independent authorization of the declared intended action</li>
      <li>Does not resume the original provider response as authority</li>
      <li>DENY prevents enterprise effect for the declared action</li>
      <li>ALLOW authorizes execution by the enterprise application only</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Verification access boundary</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard Verify is read-only. It cannot modify evidence, change a
      historical decision, activate a release, alter a Compliance Playbook,
      grant runtime authority, or authorize an enterprise action.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Verification does not participate in ALLOW or DENY</li>
      <li>Browser-side claims are untrusted for integrity conclusions</li>
      <li>Export signing and reconstruction remain server-side responsibilities</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Identity and privileged access</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard integrates with enterprise identity systems while the customer
      identity provider remains the workforce identity authority.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Federated workforce identity</li>
      <li>Service and workload identities</li>
      <li>Role and tenant boundaries</li>
      <li>Privileged step-up</li>
      <li>Session revocation</li>
      <li>Delegated administration</li>
      <li>Break-glass governance</li>
      <li>Customer identity provider remains authoritative</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Runtime isolation and replay protection</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Request authenticity</li>
      <li>Tenant binding</li>
      <li>Environment binding</li>
      <li>
        Replay resistance for governed requests where shared operation is
        configured
      </li>
      <li>Single-use authorization for review grants where applicable</li>
      <li>Fail-closed handling when required dependencies cannot be verified</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Replay resistance and isolation characteristics are deployment- and
      configuration-dependent. This site does not claim absolute multi-region
      replay guarantees.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Evidence provenance and integrity</h2>
    <p>
      Authorization decisions produce Verifiable Decision Records containing
      decision context, release identity, control result, and outcome. Related
      request and action decisions support investigation through correlation
      identifiers. Evidence supports governance and audit review; legal
      evidentiary status and regulatory admissibility are not claimed.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Evidence recorded before forward where enforcement requires it
      </li>
      <li>Decision and release identity</li>
      <li>Integrity checks on evidence and export artifacts</li>
      <li>
        Signed release artifacts (distinct from export signing and evidence-chain
        checks)
      </li>
      <li>Restart continuity where the configured evidence provider supports it</li>
      <li>Deployment-dependent retention and durability</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Reconstruction integrity</h2>
    <p>
      Deterministic reconstruction derives the logical decision path from
      canonical evidence. Missing or unavailable fields are reported as such.
      Reconstruction does not invent model reasoning or fabricate missing
      artifacts. Capability status reflects lab and prevalidation qualification.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Export integrity</h2>
    <p>
      Verifiable export packages investigation material for review. Export
      signing and verification are server-side responsibilities. Keys are not
      browser-held. Export supports investigation and audit review; court
      admissibility is not claimed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Investigation replay</h2>
    <p>
      Replay verification of recorded decisions supports investigation. It is
      distinct from admission-path replay resistance and is not cryptographic
      replay of model inference. Status reflects lab and prevalidation
      qualification.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>AI-insight non-authority</h2>
    <p>
      AI-assisted insights are a separate non-authoritative side capability.
      They must not appear inside the trusted reconstruction or runtime
      authorization path and must not be treated as evidence authority.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Release and artifact assurance</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Validated and compiled Compliance Playbooks</li>
      <li>Signed immutable release artifacts</li>
      <li>Release verification</li>
      <li>Explicit activation</li>
      <li>Active Verified Release enforcement</li>
      <li>
        Prevention of unverified or mixed release material entering the governed
        runtime path
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Operational resilience</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Controlled installation qualification</li>
      <li>Backup and restore</li>
      <li>Rollback</li>
      <li>Removal and reinstall</li>
      <li>Restart continuity</li>
      <li>Graceful shutdown behavior</li>
      <li>Controlled Windows and Linux deployment paths</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These paths are evaluated under controlled internal qualification. Public
      package downloads and general installer distribution are not implied.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Responsibility separation</h2>
    <p>
      The AI provider produces the model result. The enterprise application
      declares the intended action and executes only authorized actions.
      BeaconGuard authorizes or denies under the Active Verified Release. The
      enterprise system of record remains authoritative for business state.
      Customer governance retains clinical, legal, operational, compliance, and
      business judgment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Customer-controlled deployment responsibilities</h2>
    <p>
      Customers remain responsible for identity providers, network controls,
      secrets, host hardening, evidence-provider configuration, and operational
      monitoring. BeaconGuard provides the authorization and verification
      boundary; deployment responsibilities are shared.
    </p>
    <TrustResponsibilityMap />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Available review materials</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/docs/architecture">System Architecture</a> — available</li>
      <li><a href="/how-it-works">How It Works</a> — available</li>
      <li><a href="/verification">Verification</a> — available</li>
      <li><a href="/proof-evidence">Proof and Evidence samples</a> — sample</li>
      <li><a href="/sample-decision-record">Sample Decision Record</a> — sample</li>
      <li><a href="/control-responsibility-matrix">Control Responsibility Matrix</a> — available</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/threat-model">Threat Model</a> — available</li>
      <li><a href="/reviewer-kit">Reviewer Kit</a> — available</li>
      <li>
        Customer-specific architecture-fit materials — controlled-access via
        Controlled Evaluation
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Security contact</h2>
    <p>
      For architecture and security review inquiries, contact
      <a href="mailto:architecture@beaconguardassurance.com">architecture@beaconguardassurance.com</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Compliance and certification status</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard publishes technical architecture and controlled review
      materials for due diligence. This site does not claim SOC 2, ISO 27001,
      HITRUST, HIPAA certification, FedRAMP authorization, or similar
      attestation status. Ask during Controlled Evaluation for the current
      status of any formal assessment work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Evidence and legal boundaries</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Evidence supports governance and audit review; legal evidentiary status
        and regulatory admissibility are not claimed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Append-only or durable retention applies only where the configured
        evidence provider and operational controls provide those guarantees.
      </li>
      <li>
        BeaconGuard does not replace customer systems of record or compliance
        programs.
      </li>
      <li>
        BeaconGuard enforcement results are deterministic ALLOW or DENY
        outcomes, not probabilistic authorization.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/platform">Review platform assumptions</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/contact">Discuss review expectations</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
